.TH RUNEOPT 1 "2026" "runeopt 0.3.0" "Rune Manual"
.SH NAME
runeopt \- translate Rune bytecode into a native program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B runeopt
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-S ]
.RB [ \-\-options
.IR text ]
.I file.rbc
.br
.B runeopt
.RB ( \-\-check | \-\-facts )
.IR file.rbc " ..."
.br
.B runeopt
.RB ( \-\-disasm | \-\-lines )
.I file.rbc
.br
.B runeopt \-\-inlined
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B runeopt
translates a bytecode file made by
.BR rune (1)
into an executable for Linux on x86-64: every instruction becomes the machine
code that does what
.BR runevm (1)
does for it, in the order of the bytecode, and the C compiler assembles the
code and links it with the runtime of
.BR runevm .
The program keeps the value stack, the frames and the handlers where
.B runevm
keeps them, so it prints what it prints under
.BR runevm ,
counts the same instructions, bytes and objects, reports an uncaught
exception and a trace in the same words, and writes the same images with
.BR Runtime.save .
The common case of the arithmetic and comparisons of integers, words, reals
and characters, of equality on values that are not pointers, of references
and of the elements of strings, vectors and arrays is done by the code
itself; everything else calls the primitive
.B runevm
calls.
.PP
The executable carries its bytecode and a line table: a debugger stops at a
line of an SML source and shows the function, which is named by its name in
the source and its number,
.IR name # n .
.PP
A file that the loader of
.B runevm
refuses is refused with its message, and so is one whose code does not keep
what the translation relies on (the compiler's code always does): the stack
has the same height on every path into an instruction, no jump leaves its
function, and no function runs off its end.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
The executable to write. The default is the name of the bytecode file without
.BR .rbc .
.TP
.B \-S
Write the assembly, to
.I file
or the name of the bytecode file with
.BR .s ,
and stop.
.TP
.BI \-\-options " text"
Options of
.B runevm
the program is to run with, written as
.B runevm
takes them:
.BR \-\-count ,
.BR \-\-stats ,
.BI \-\-heap\-size " N"
and
.BI \-\-gc\-stress " N."
Those of
.B RUNEVM_OPTIONS
come after these.
.TP
.BI \-\-cc " cc"
The C compiler that assembles and links (default
.BR cc ).
.TP
.BI \-\-runtime " dir"
Where the runtime is:
.B librune.a
and
.BR rune\-offsets.s .
The installed
.B runeopt
passes it.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Check each file, and translate nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-disasm
Print the bytecode as
.B runevm \-\-disasm
prints it.
.TP
.B \-\-lines
Print the line table of the file: the pc, file, line and column of each entry.
.TP
.B \-\-facts
Print what the check found in each file: functions, instructions, the
highest stack, and the places the code resumes at.
.TP
.B \-\-inlined
List the primitives whose common case the code does itself.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
A program
.B runeopt
made takes the options of
.B runevm
from
.BR RUNEVM_OPTIONS ,
after those it was made with, and removes the variable from its environment,
so that it sees the environment it has under
.BR runevm .
Its
.B CommandLine.name
is its own name, or
.B RUNEVM_NAME
where that is set, which the test suites use.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B runeopt
exits 0 when it wrote what it was asked to, and 1 when a file is refused, when
the options are wrong, or when the C compiler fails. A program it made exits
as it would under
.BR runevm .
.SH BUGS
.B Runtime.restore
raises
.B OS.SysErr
in a native program, and a native program cannot carry on an image, nor be
the child of a fork emulated by
.BR \-\-emulate\-fork ;
.B runevm \-\-restore
carries on an image a native program saved.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rune (1),
.BR runevm (1)
